(Description)
Given an array nums of distinct integers, return all the possible permutations. You can return the answer in any order.

--- Solution ---
class Solution {
    List<List<Integer>> result;
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        result = new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        findOrder(nums, new ArrayList<>(), new int[nums.length]);
        return result;
    }

    private void findOrder(int[] nums, List<Integer> order, int[] road) {
        if (order.size() == nums.length) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(order));
            return;
        }
        
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i ++) {
            if(road[i] == 0) {
                road[i] = 1;
                order.add(nums[i]);

                findOrder(nums, order, road);

                order.remove(order.size() - 1);
                road[i] = 0;
            }
        }
    }
}

--- Approach ---
Key point in this algorithm is the check box of int array.
The cehck box have same legth of the given array, filling all as 0 at the beginning, changed its value to the 1 if the int was added at the same position in the given array.

Then, in the recursion step, algorithm look for the checkbox,
1. if current value is 1, it skips because the int at the position was added already,
2. but if the value is 0, it adds the int at the position.

[Improvement]
After completing this problem, I recognized that this solution is not efficient in enough.
Because in the step of opening the check box, it uses for loop statment from beginning to the end.
If it can skip the positions that was added already, it might save more time.
